ction of purpose and  in that purpose we in fact I wrote it so  I state that it is not the purpose of  this document and all this work you have  to do and all this training you have to  do to ensure you get enough water  wherever you want as much as you want  for however long as you want words to  that effect it is certainly a document  to help you maintain your manufacturing  operations and sustain them but it  recognizes limits it says that this is a  shared environment and therefore there  are limits in in the physical nature as  well as as competing uses of that water  that may have higher priority so right  there we're acknowledging in the very  core of how we communicate and govern  this program internally with saying that  that's the underlying premise for  approaching this so but if it could have  changed a business model I don't know so  maybe I can ask jocelyn a related  question you thinking about sustaining  over time and the impact of climate  change and the sort of water energy  nexus and i gather that some of your  thinking about water requirements and  water footprinting is  been linked to renewable energy credits  yes where did that come from yeah very  much so so so obviously it takes a lot  of electricity to move water around  right that's a given and then as we got  into some of the deeper analysis in our  footprinting and in our strategy what we  really started focusing on was how much  water it takes to produce electricity  and so overall again focusing on the  energy efficiency of our operations the  energy efficiency that our products can  ultimately deliver to our end users when  you look at something like a data center  it's all obviously very interconnected  and we do look at it in a very  integrated holistic way I yeah I think  that's in general that's definitely  something that we've kind of shifted to  over the past couple of years yep maybe  one last question started in the hi my  name is corey johnson i'm here at the  forestry school 